\subsection{Pixel-Shuffle Entanglement}
\label{sec:pixel_shuffle}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_pixel_shuffle.pdf}
\caption{Pixel-shuffle $4{\times}4$ folding: each block of 16 SigLIP patches is channel-concatenated into one token. Removing any token eliminates a non-overlapping $4{\times}4$ receptive field (gray) with no redundancy from neighboring tokens.}
\label{fig:pixel_shuffle}
\end{figure}

SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle $4{\times}4$ folding concatenates 16 patches per token (Figure~\ref{fig:pixel_shuffle}), creating non-overlapping receptive fields with no inter-token redundancy. SmolVLA collapses to ${\leq}$6.6\% SR at $k{=}0.95$ across all suites and methods, verified on two checkpoints (44.8\% and 59.2\% baselines); the cliff is architectural. No scoring function can identify dispensable tokens because all carry spatially entangled information; the failure is that any removal breaks completeness. Token-independent connectors retain per-patch locality, so partial removal preserves spatial coverage.

\subsection{When Does Pruning Method Matter? CV as Sensitivity Indicator}
\label{sec:cv_sensitivity}

Token importance CV (Table~\ref{tab:model_properties}) correlates with the retention ratio at which pruning method choice becomes critical. At $k{=}0.95$, we compare the three closed-loop models with nonzero SR.

For $\pi_{0.5}$ (CV$\,{=}\,$0.055), the method gap at $k{=}0.95$ is substantial on Object (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}): L2 retains the full baseline (98.2\%) while random selection degrades markedly. Near-uniform norms cause L2 ranking to exploit spatial correlations rather than genuine importance, while random selection preserves the uniform coverage these models depend on.

For X-VLA (CV$\,{=}\,$0.151), the gap averages 0.8\,pp across all four suites (range 0.2--1.4\,pp).

For OpenVLA (CV$\,{=}\,$0.215), the method gap at $k{=}0.9$ is suite-dependent: L2 outperforms random by $+$7.8\,pp on Object (70.6\% vs.\ 62.8\%) but underperforms by $-$4.8\,pp on Spatial (75.0\% vs.\ 79.8\%), while both methods match baseline on Goal (${\leq}$4.8\,pp gap). This within-model crossover demonstrates that scoring validity depends on task semantics even for a single connector type.

X-VLA shows method gaps ${\leq}$1.4\,pp at $k{=}0.95$ across all four suites (Figure~\ref{fig:method_gap}). OpenVLA's suite-dependent method gap---ranging from $+$7.8\,pp (L2 favored) to $-$4.8\,pp (random favored)---illustrates that higher CV enables but does not guarantee L2 advantage.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_method_gap.pdf}
\caption{Method gap $|\text{SR(L2)} - \text{SR(Random)}|$ at $k{=}0.95$. $\pi_{0.5}$'s Object gap (24.2\,pp) exceeds X-VLA's maximum (1.4\,pp) by $17{\times}$, reflecting CV-correlated divergence in pruning method sensitivity.}
\label{fig:method_gap}
\end{figure}

At $k{=}0.5$, method-independence breaks down: $\Delta$(R$-$L2) on Object is $+$34.8\,pp ($\pi_{0.5}$), $+$27.2\,pp (X-VLA), $-$3.4\,pp (OpenVLA). Random preserves spatial diversity; L2 clusters correlated tokens, exploiting the genuine gradient only for CV$\,{>}\,$0.2.

In practice, CV partitions models into three compressibility classes: \emph{uniform} (CV$\,{<}\,$0.1; safe for moderate pruning but method-sensitive), \emph{graded} (CV$\,{\approx}\,$0.15--0.22; threshold-dependent degradation, L2 increasingly effective with higher CV), and \emph{catastrophic} (CV$\,{>}\,$0.3; no pruning tolerance). This coarse classification, validated on four closed-loop architectures, provides actionable guidance without requiring downstream evaluation.

\textbf{Architecture-dependent importance scoring.} Extending to attention-based scoring on X-VLA ($k{=}0.5$) reveals that scoring validity depends on connector architecture (Table~\ref{tab:attention_pruning}). L2 norm falls below random on all four suites ($-$8 to $-$27\,pp), while attention scoring recovers near-baseline performance (${\geq}$92.8\%, peaking at 99.2\% on Object). The BART encoder's cross-token self-attention distributes information uniformly, rendering L2 norms anti-informative: Goal L2 is non-monotonic ($k{=}0.7$: 53.4\% $<$ $k{=}0.5$: 59.0\%), confirming that retaining more L2-selected tokens is actively harmful. By contrast, on $\pi_{0.5}$'s linear connector, attention is ineffective: neutral on three suites ($\pm$3\,pp vs.\ random) but catastrophically harmful on Object ($-$30.4\,pp), where attention scores select semantically relevant tokens that lack the spatial precision required for object discrimination. Meanwhile, the L2 importance margin decays from $+$35.6\,pp ($k{=}0.85$) to $+$5.6\,pp ($k{=}0.95$), a connector-dependent scoring reversal (Table~\ref{tab:scoring_crossover}; Appendix~\ref{app:attention_pruning}). OpenVLA's MLP connector reinforces the token-independent pattern on Object ($+$7.8\,pp for L2 at $k{=}0.9$), but the advantage reverses on Spatial ($-$4.8\,pp), showing that scoring validity is jointly determined by connector architecture and task semantics.

\textbf{Disruption peak in random pruning.} Random pruning on $\pi_{0.5}$ is surprisingly unreliable at conservative retention ratios, with task-dependent severity. At $k{=}0.85$, random selection drops to 52.4\% on Goal, 56.6\% on Spatial, and 3.6--5.6\% on Object (two seeds), all below their $k{=}0.5$ performance (73.6\%, 71.6\%, 68.2\% respectively). The Object collapse is confirmed across two seeds and independent RNG implementations (seed42: 5.6\%, seed43: 3.6\%; CI overlap), ruling out both sampling noise and RNG artifacts. L2 selection shows no such valley: Object L2 at $k{=}0.85$ retains 99.0\%, yielding an importance margin exceeding $+$90\,pp, the strongest in our study. The disruption window at ${\sim}$15\% removal falls in a critical regime where enough tokens are displaced to break $\pi_{0.5}$'s learned positional computation patterns, but too few are removed to trigger the robust degradation mode observed at $k{=}0.5$. L2 scoring avoids this vulnerability entirely, demonstrating that importance-based selection is not merely a marginal improvement but a qualitative necessity at intermediate pruning ratios.

\textbf{Importance margin gradient.} The L2 importance margin---the advantage of L2 over random at $k{=}0.85$---decreases monotonically with task complexity on $\pi_{0.5}$: Object $+$93\,pp (99.0\% vs.\ 5.6\%) $>$ Goal $+$35.2\,pp $>$ Long $+$28.6\,pp (78.0\% vs.\ 49.4\%). As compositional demands increase, L2 norms become a weaker proxy for token utility: multi-step tasks distribute critical information across tokens with heterogeneous norm profiles, compressing the margin between importance-based and random selection.

\textbf{Per-task reversal within L2-dominant regimes.} The Long $k{=}0.85$ aggregate ($+$28.6\,pp for L2) masks extreme per-task heterogeneity (per-task $\sigma{=}$21.5\,pp). L2 outperforms random on 9 of 10 tasks ($+$16 to $+$52\,pp), but on Task~6---a dual-object spatial-relation task (``place white mug on plate \emph{and} chocolate pudding to the right of plate'')---L2 collapses to 8\% while random achieves 52\% ($-$44\,pp reversal). L2 scoring systematically undervalues tokens encoding spatial relationships, which carry low norms but are critical for relational reasoning. This per-task sign flip within an architecture where L2 dominates overall reinforces that no single scoring method is universally safe: task semantics, not just connector architecture or suite-level statistics, determine optimal strategy.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Scoring ${\times}$ connector-dependent scoring reversal ($\Delta$ vs.\ random at $k{=}0.5$, except \textsuperscript{*}Goal suite across $k \in [0.85, 0.95]$).}
\label{tab:scoring_crossover}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
& Attention & L2 norm \\
\midrule
Entangling (X-VLA) & Effective ($+$31\,pp) & Anti-inform.\ ($-$8 to $-$27\,pp) \\
Token-indep.\ ($\pi_{0.5}$) & Ineffective\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} ($-$2 to $-$30\,pp) & Effective\textsuperscript{*} ($+$35.6$\to$$+$5.6\,pp) \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\footnotesize\textsuperscript{$\dagger$}Neutral on 3/4 suites ($\pm$3\,pp); harmful on Object ($-$30.4\,pp).}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{VTR Recovery}
\label{sec:vtr_recovery}

Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR) addresses the catastrophic cliff through learned re-encoding rather than hard deletion. A cross-attention module (${\sim}$22M parameters, ${\sim}$5\% of SmolVLA) uses $M$ learned queries to attend over all 128 source tokens, recovering SmolVLA from 0.0\% to over 60\% SR at 75\% compression ($M{=}32$: 60.1 $\pm$ 1.3\%, 3 seeds). The M-curve is flat ($M{=}16$ matches $M{=}128$ within 1.1\,pp), and a parameter-matched MLP adapter achieves comparable performance (63.4 $\pm$ 2.6\%, $p{=}0.15$), confirming the gain stems from re-encoding itself. Cross-suite generalization: Spatial 53.2\%, Goal 56.4\%, both from 0.0\%. Training uses batch size 4 on one GPU (vs.\ 512 across 8 GPUs officially); the lower baseline (44.8\% vs.\ 59.2\%) reflects this gap (Appendix~\ref{app:smart_compress_details},~\ref{app:vtr_full}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{VTR re-encoding on SmolVLA (LIBERO-Object). Hard pruning collapses all configurations to 0\%; VTR recovers ${\geq}$57\% at up to 87.5\% compression. MLP adapter ($p{=}0.15$ vs.\ cross-attention) confirms re-encoding, not cross-attention specifically, drives the gain. Full results in Appendix~\ref{app:vtr_full}.}
\label{tab:vtr_main}
\begin{tabular}{lccr}
\toprule
Configuration & $M$ & Compress.\ & SR (\%) \\
\midrule
\textit{Baseline (unpruned)} & 128 & 0\% & 44.8 \\
\textit{Hard pruning (any)} & -- & 5--50\% & 0.0 \\
\midrule
Cross-attn VTR & 32 & 75\% & 60.1 $\pm$ 1.3 \\
Cross-attn VTR & 16 & 87.5\% & 56.9 $\pm$ 1.3 \\
MLP adapter & 32 & 75\% & 63.4 $\pm$ 2.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Limitations.}
All evaluations use LIBERO simulation; we provide no sim-to-real evidence. The six architectures differ in backbone, encoder, and scale beyond the connector, preventing causal isolation. The CV--SR correlation relies on four closed-loop models, too few for a general law. VTR is validated on SmolVLA only. Most conditions use a single seed; cross-seed validation shows $\Delta \leq 5.8$\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:pi05_multiseed},~\ref{app:openvla_multiseed}). Extended discussion in Appendix~\ref{app:discussion}.

