\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:exp_setup}

We evaluate vision token pruning across six VLA architectures spanning three connector families on the LIBERO benchmark~\cite{liu2023libero}. LIBERO provides four task suites of increasing complexity: Object (object manipulation), Spatial (spatial reasoning), Goal (goal-conditioned tasks), and Long (long-horizon multi-step tasks). Each suite contains 10 tasks evaluated over 50 episodes, yielding 500 episodes per condition.

Four models are evaluated in closed-loop robotic control: $\pi_{0.5}$~\cite{black2025pi05} (4B parameters, linear projection with $\times\!\sqrt{d}$ scaling, 768 tokens from three cameras, CV$\,{=}\,$0.055), X-VLA~\cite{zheng2025xvla} (880M, linear projection followed by a 12-layer BART encoder~\cite{lewis2020bart}, 50 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.151), OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024openvla} (7B, three-layer MLP projector, 256 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.215), and SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} (450M, pixel-shuffle $4{\times}4$ folding, 128 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.314). Two additional models are analyzed via offline metrics only: $\pi_{0}$~\cite{black2024pi0} (3.3B, linear projection, 256 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.038) and OFT~\cite{kim2025openoft} (7B, three-layer MLP, 512 tokens, CV$\,{=}\,$0.219). All six architectures contribute to the CV diagnostic (Section~\ref{sec:cv_sensitivity}). Table~\ref{tab:model_properties} summarizes model properties and connector details.

We follow the pruning protocol of Section~\ref{sec:pruning_protocol} at retention ratios $k \in \{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95\}$, reporting mean success rate (SR) over 500 episodes with Wilson 95\% confidence intervals.

\subsection{Two-Axis Framework Overview}
\label{sec:two_axis}

The pruning response depends on token independence imposed by the connector and task complexity (Figure~\ref{fig:teaser}). Token-independent connectors degrade gracefully; token-entangling connectors exhibit sharp, task-dependent collapse.

